MASTER IMPLEMENTATION PROMPT

ALVA: CPU Intelligence — Complete AI Replacement System

1. Mission

You are a senior game AI engineer, combat-systems architect, and competitive platform-fighting-game designer.

Your task is to design, implement, integrate, test, and optimize a completely new CPU opponent system for ALVA called CPU Intelligence.

Repository: hiyroscript/alva

This is a full replacement of the current combat CPU intelligence, not an adjustment, patch, or extension of its existing decision-making.

The new system must create opponents that feel like real human players across all four difficulties, with intelligent movement, positioning, reactions, combos, defense, stage awareness, and tactical decisions.

The CPU must understand what it can do, what its opponent can do, the circumstances of the fight, and why a particular action is useful.

It should not simply react to distance thresholds or randomly select moves.

The desired result is an opponent that appears to be thinking, adapting, making plans, executing strategies, recognizing opportunities, and learning from its opponent during a match.

Most importantly: implement one universal intelligence system that automatically understands and plays every character through existing character and move data.

Do not create separate CPU brains, decision trees, hardcoded move scripts, or custom strategic logic for individual characters.

The difficulty levels must share the same fundamental intelligence architecture. They differ in how effectively they observe, anticipate, evaluate, react, plan, adapt, and execute.

The four difficulties represent:

Difficulty	Intended skill
Easy	Casual human player
Medium	Competitive tryhard player
Hard	Tournament/professional-level player
Brutal	Elite player with a target win rate of 95% or higher against a defined competitive benchmark

A CPU on Easy must still feel like a human playing the game. It should simply be noticeably less skilled than the higher difficulties.

A CPU on Brutal must be extremely difficult to defeat through superior decision-making, without cheating.

Do not stop at a design document or prototype. Implement the working system in the repository, integrate it into the game, and validate it.

