3. Absolute Non-Negotiable Requirements

Rule A — CPUs must never become inactive

On every difficulty, a CPU must never stand still doing nothing while it is free to act and the match is active.

This includes Easy.

The CPU must always have a purposeful objective or meaningful ongoing action.

Examples include:

* Advancing toward an opponent.
* Retreating to a safer position.
* Maintaining advantageous spacing.
* Moving to a better stage location.
* Jumping or changing elevation.
* Preparing an attack or combo.
* Shielding an actual threat.
* Baiting the opponent while maintaining active positioning.
* Recovering to the stage.
* Repositioning while the opponent respawns.
* Managing distance while Energy or Launch Point recovers.

Do not implement random movement just to satisfy activity. The movement must have a tactical purpose.

A brief natural pause, deliberate bait, or held defensive position is acceptable when it is part of a meaningful plan. Prolonged neutral inactivity is not.

Stun, hitstop, paralysis, forced attack animations, respawn transitions, match introductions, and other states where the fighter cannot act are exceptions. These are physical game restrictions, not failures of intelligence.

Create an action-liveness system that detects and corrects empty, expired, failed, or stuck plans.

There should be no generic idle intent that causes CPUs to repeatedly wait without a purpose.

Rule B — Every available move must be used

Every CPU, on every difficulty, must reasonably utilize its character’s complete valid moveset.

This includes:

* Every numbered attack available to that character.
* Grounded attacks.
* Aerial attacks.
* Extra attacks.
* Summons.
* Techniques.
* Projectiles.
* Movement-based attacks.
* Deflect as an offensive attack.
* Deflect as a projectile counter.
* Other legitimate character abilities.

If a character has five numbered attacks, the CPU must understand all five.

If another character has three, it must understand those three.

A ground-only move must never be attempted in the air. An unavailable or reserved action must never be pressed.

Move utilization must be intelligent, not arbitrary.

Do not force a dangerous move into an obviously inappropriate situation merely to increase usage statistics.

Instead, create conditions, setups, and strategies where different moves become worthwhile.

If an attack rarely gets chosen because another attack always scores higher, improve the tactical evaluation and combo/setup planning rather than inserting meaningless randomness.

Track move usage and opportunity coverage during testing.

Every supported move must have at least one meaningful scenario where the intelligence recognizes and executes it.

Rule C — Universal intelligence

Never branch on fighter IDs such as 0001, 0002, or specific character names.

Never write a character-specific CPU brain.

Never implement bespoke instructions such as “if fighter 0001, always use attack4 against shielding opponents.”

Instead, recognize the generic properties of the move: for example, an unblockable area technique that can punish a stationary shielding opponent.

The same logic must work for any future fighter with comparable capabilities.

Rule D — No cheating

The CPU must obey exactly the same combat and movement rules as the human player.

No increased damage, artificial launch power, faster movement, hidden Energy, ignored recovery, shortened cooldowns, extra jumps, impossible inputs, or special invulnerability.

All decisions must ultimately become legitimate player-compatible commands through the controller.

Do not directly alter fighter position, velocity, cooldowns, hit results, combat state, or projectiles.

Do not inspect the player’s raw controller inputs to predict attacks before the moves become observable.

Brutal must be powerful because it makes better decisions, not because it has privileges.

