6. Intelligent Launch Point and Knockback Management

This is one of the most important parts of CPU Intelligence.

ALVA uses Launch Point rather than conventional health.

The existing mechanics must be respected:

* Launch Point begins at 0.
* Unblocked damage adds to Launch Point.
* The hit’s damage is applied before calculating its launch.
* Launch strength = Base Launch × the defender’s new Launch Point.
* Base Launch values are 0, 1, 2, and 3.
* Launch directions are horizontal, vertical, reverse vertical, or none.
* The actual launch velocity follows the shared launch conversion and physics.
* Launch Point begins recovering after the prescribed no-hit delay.

Create a risk model that considers both fighters’ current Launch Points.

A CPU must understand that higher Launch Point increases danger even when the next attack deals relatively little damage.

Own Launch Point

When the CPU’s own Launch Point rises:

* Become more aware of elimination threats.
* Avoid unnecessarily standing near dangerous ledges.
* Value safe ground and escape routes.
* Recognize which opposing moves have dangerous launch strength and direction.
* Defend more intelligently rather than shielding everything.
* Create space when temporary disengagement is advantageous.
* Let Launch Point recover when it can do so safely.
* Reduce high-risk commitments when those commitments could lead to elimination.
* Preserve offensive pressure when it offers a safer option than retreat.

Do not implement a simplistic threshold where high Launch Point always means running away.

Opponent Launch Point

When the opponent’s Launch Point rises:

* Recognize finishing opportunities.
* Increase the value of appropriate high-Base-Launch moves.
* Prefer launches that lead toward nearby Void boundaries.
* Position to control the opponent’s escape direction.
* Use combo routes that set up reliable finishers.
* Edge-guard when the opponent is vulnerable.
* Stop unnecessarily choosing low-impact moves when a credible finishing opportunity exists.

Dynamic playstyle transitions

The CPU should smoothly transition between:

* Extremely offensive.
* Aggressively controlled.
* Balanced.
* Defensive counterplay.
* Survival-oriented.
* Opportunistic finishing.

The transition should consider both fighters’ Launch Points, position, momentum, available abilities, Energy, stage hazards, score, and time.

Two fighters with equally high Launch Points should not necessarily play alike. Their current positions, movesets, and defensive options matter.

Use a continuous risk/reward model with hysteresis rather than rapid, unrealistic switching every frame.

