9. Positioning, Footwork, and Neutral Game

The CPU must be intelligent even when nobody is attacking.

Create a continuous neutral-game system that maintains useful positioning.

It must understand:

* Its own effective attack ranges.
* The opponent’s effective attack ranges.
* Safe and unsafe spacing.
* Threat ranges including the opponent’s attack movement.
* Approaching versus retreating.
* Facing and movement momentum.
* Height advantage and disadvantage.
* Ground versus air control.
* Ledge proximity.
* Projectile lanes.
* Available cover or obstacles.
* Whether an opponent is approaching, retreating, shielding, or committing.

Possible behaviors include:

Spacing: remain outside an opponent’s dangerous range while preserving the ability to punish.

Whiff baiting: move close enough to encourage an opponent to attack, retreat before contact, then punish the recovery.

Approaching: close distance through a safe route when the CPU needs to initiate offense.

Corner pressure: restrict an opponent’s room without carelessly walking into the Void.

Kiting: maintain distance when ranged attacks, recovery, or survival justify it.

Crossing levels: use platforms and jump paths to reach an opponent at a different elevation.

Repositioning: change position when the current location offers no useful tactical advantage.

The CPU must not run directly into the opponent indefinitely.

It must not repeatedly walk back and forth without purpose.

It must not continuously jump just to look active.

It must not remain outside fighting range for an entire match when it has a viable offensive path.

Every movement decision should support a recognizable short-term or long-term goal.

