24. Predictive Simulation and Tactical Search

Develop a lightweight prediction system using actual game mechanics.

It should estimate:

* Future fighter positions.
* Attack hitbox arrival.
* Projectile interception.
* Landing positions.
* Jump trajectories.
* Dash travel.
* Move-induced displacement.
* Launch trajectories.
* Ledge crossing.
* Recovery feasibility.
* Combo connection probability.
* Punishable recovery windows.

Do not build a second physics engine with different rules.

Reuse shared mechanics and pure functions where practical.

A limited-depth search or utility-based action planner is preferred over massive hardcoded condition trees.

The search can use a reduced state representation rather than cloning the full game.

Account for uncertainty.

A prediction based on an opponent continuing its current movement is not a guarantee the opponent will do so.

Brutal may analyze more candidate sequences and longer horizons.

Easy may use shorter lookahead and less accurate predictions.

All difficulties should still recognize the same kinds of tactical possibilities.

