26. Recovery and Survival Intelligence

Offstage recovery deserves its own high-priority planner.

The CPU must assess:

* Whether it is currently above or below the stage surface.
* Distance from the nearest safe landing position.
* Horizontal and vertical momentum.
* Number of remaining jumps.
* Air Dash availability.
* Remaining Energy.
* Available movement-based attacks.
* Free-fall restrictions.
* Launch-reaction restrictions.
* Opponent interference.
* Current Void boundaries.

Plan a recovery route using legal movement.

Potential actions:

* Drift toward the stage.
* Use available air jumps.
* Air Dash home when suitable.
* Use a valid rising or movement-based attack when it improves recovery.
* Adjust horizontal positioning before landing.
* Avoid an opponent’s edge-guarding attack.
* Choose the safest reachable landing zone.

Do not use every jump immediately when spacing their use provides a better recovery.

Do not save Energy at the cost of certain elimination.

Do not activate a free-fall-producing move when it eliminates the remaining recovery route unless it is the best available option.

The current CPU’s survival behavior must be improved, not simply copied.

Recovery should be a competence shared by every difficulty, with increasing accuracy.

